{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1146716-can-someone-help-me-make-this-servitor-using-sphere-magic","id":1146716,"title":"Can someone help me make this servitor using Sphere magic?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-10-04T10:24:21-07:00","id":1146716,"title":"Can someone help me make this servitor using Sphere magic?","content":"I'm talking with a player who wants to make a character that is based on using &quot;real world occultism&quot;/magic. The thing is, many of the &quot;real wizards,&quot; he's talked to keep talking about making Servitors, and how its one of the easier things to do. <br /> Now, I'm not here to discuss such things being real or make believe, or any of that. I just want to help the player figure out how viable this is using Sphere magic. Don't feel the need to read the whole article, but at least skim through it and get a sense of what the Servitor is. Then, come back here and lets figure out how to make it work.<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://johnkreiter.com/how-to-create-a-servitor-to-do-your-bidding/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://johnkreiter.com/wp-content/uploads/logo.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://johnkreiter.com/how-to-create-a-servitor-to-do-your-bidding/\" target=\"_blank\"> How to Create a Servitor to Do Your Bidding &ndash; John Kreiter </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://johnkreiter.com/how-to-create-a-servitor-to-do-your-bidding/\" target=\"_blank\"> http://johnkreiter.com/how-to-create-a-servitor-to-do-your-bidding/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6010-shadowstep6","name":"Shadowstep6","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-04T10:31:01-07:00","id":1146720,"title":"","content":"Spirit 5 or gaining a Familiar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-04T10:48:58-07:00","id":1146727,"title":"","content":"I kinda thought the same thing, but the fact that its created from your own spirit or personality implies Prime or Mind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6010-shadowstep6","name":"Shadowstep6","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-05T07:19:59-07:00","id":1147028,"title":"","content":"Servitors, Tulpas...<br /> <br /> I'm no expert, but I think that it should be lower than creating a true spirit or AI. Servitors have no self-will unless you botch (then they start becoming self conscious which it's a problem), so I think you could treat it as a small construct of your mind and spirit that has a certain autonomy.<br /> <br /> A few posibilities:<br /> <br /> * The Familiar or Spirit Ally Background. Say that when you Awakened, you crafted a &quot;dream spirit&quot; (from your own subconscious) with your Wild Magic (Handwave it). Now it serves you.<br /> <br /> *You could use this as a bizarre, but plausible, &quot;Instrument&quot;. Servitors are often aids to your magical stuff, thus, you could use Meditation or Mind to create a sort of potent &quot;tought construct&quot;, and then say that you're using that mental image as &quot;a servitor&quot; that it's the excuse you use to do magick<br /> <br /> *Maybe you can interpret the Spheres this way: With Mind 3 you access your own subconscious/dreamscape, there you create a small mental construct with Mind 3/Prime 2 (&quot;dream&quot; it into being with Meditation, using a little magick to help fuel it's pattern), and, perhaps, Awaken it with Spirit 3. The idea it's to craft something that you may be able to summon afterwards using Spirit 2. It won't have any Charms right out of the bat (it's more like an Awakened object than a Spirit you could craft with Spirit 5), but it will be inclined to serve you.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind you may not be able to do right out the bat everything than a real mystic finds &quot;easy&quot;...Spheres can't be perfect at representing real world occultism because there's no real world &quot;unified system of magick&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 10-05-2017, 10:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-05T10:19:24-07:00","id":1147098,"title":"","content":"Easiest method seems to be to use the Familiar background and say they created it (perhaps when they awakened as Aleph suggested). It's a simple solution and there's no need to worry about the Spheres involved unless they plan on making another, in which case you can make that a medium to long term goal for the character to accomplish.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-05T17:41:36-07:00","id":1147255,"title":"","content":"Honestly, you might be overcomplicating things. Just say the &quot;Servitor&quot; is a Thought Form. It doesn't have any more existence except what the user believes they have. In practical matters, they're an imagined figure used as an Instrument for pulling off Effects. A mental construct that does the heavy lifting for the mage, but may or may not exist outside of them. At least, no more than the &quot;spirits&quot; a shaman invokes when performing Effects that don't use the Spirit Sphere. Just like said shaman might ask the spirit in a door to unlock, it doesn't matter if the Thought Form Servitor actually exists as a being in the spiritual or mental realms. It only matters if the mage believes enough that they do to accomplish magick through them.<br /> <br /> Which doesn't mean you can't make a &quot;real&quot; Servitor with Sphere magick. I've argued before on this board that the ability to create intelligences really should start at Mind 3, working up to Mind 5 with respects to how sophisticated the created mind is. At Mind 3, you have an expert system, obeying a set of instructions according to pre-chosen conditions, but unable to act outside of this. Mind 4 gives you animal level sentience; complex instincts and rich emotions, but little capacity for reason. And Mind 5 grants the ability to generate Sapience - the true intelligence, capable of logical deduction and abstract thought. I've also argued that you should be able to combine these with other Spheres, in order to give intelligence to creatures, objects, computers (in the form of AI), and even Forces.<br /> <br /> If we follow this option, you could have the mage create a Servitor using Mind 3, and either have it running in her mind, have it exist separately in the astral penumbra, or affix it to a vessel or another Effect.<br /> <br /> * In the first case, she might have a Servitor keeping an eye on her thoughts, essentially creating a guard dog against potential invaders or mental manipulation (or insanity, if the mage feared such a thing). She might make one to remind her of things, to inform her when her actions would violate her morality or ethics, or to even take limited control of her body if she goes dream walking or astral projecting (though what such a simple Servitor could do would be limited; fighting wouldn't be possible at Mind 3, but seeing to basic bodily needs or speaking pre-made messages would be possible).<br /> <br /> * In the second case, the Servitor could be left to watch a location, or to fly through the Umbra and deliver a message (though only in the astral Umbra, since it has no spiritual presence without Spirit). If the mage astral projects to the High Umbra, they might fashion a Servitor to perform simple tasks, like take notes, hold spiritual or mental objects, or transcribe texts from an astral library. All of this from Mind 3, under my option.<br /> <br /> * In the third case, you could put a Servitor in an object, like a held item, a statue, a painting, a magic book, etc. Putting an intelligence - even a limited one with a set list of if/then or do/while conditions - could be powerful. Moreso if you add additional Effects. That painting might have included a Matter 2 Effect to make it alter its appearance, so it looks like the painting is looking around, talking, making gestures, etc, while Forces 3 + Prime 2 allows it to speak. That statue might have a Matter 3 put on it, so it comes to life and interacts with people (though actually fighting would require Mind 4, since an expert system is too limited for on-the-spot judgments and reactions). A pen could be animated with Forces 3 + Prime 2 to transcribe the mage's speech. In any case, putting the Servitor in an object means it doesn't clutter up the mage's own head, and can be left running without the user needing to be there, use Correspondence, and/or keep concentrating. The Servitor will do as instructed for as long as it can, for as long as is applicable, no matter where the mage is. (If also means the Servitor won't dissolve in the mental ether, as free-floating intelligences with no tethers are wont to do, given enough time).<br /> <br /> If the above &quot;vessel&quot; is a creature, apply all that, but add that the organism can do what it can normally do, and can be made to do more with other Spheres.<br /> <br /> * In the last case, you can affix the Servitor to any random Effect, granting an intelligence of sorts to something that otherwise only exists as a spell. A Forces 3 + Prime 2 spell, for instance, that turns a general telekinesis spell into an Unseen Servant. Not tied to anything in particular, but just a force that obeys its programming and applies itself accordingly. Or a bad luck curse that goes off when the Servitor notices certain conditions are met. (Yes, this application of Mind would compete roughly with Time 4 + Entropy 3's ability to suspend an Effect until certain events come to pass. My version is an alternative method that Mages could use, if they are specialists in Mind rather than Time and Entropy).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-10-05T17:46:38-07:00","id":1147258,"title":"","content":"To continue, if a Servitor is &quot;merely&quot; a Thought Form acting as an Instrument, it would simplify what it means for the Servitor to &quot;go out of control&quot; or &quot;gain too much autonomy&quot;.<br /> <br /> It just means the mage has suffered a Paradox backlash, which manifests as their Servitor acting against them, or in a way she didn't intend. A Burn might involve the Servitor attacking her, while a Flaw might be the Servitor performing its function inappropriately. A Paradox Spirit could be the Thought Form gaining physical form, while a Quiet might be the Servitor turning into a Hobgoblin that torments its creator.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}